So the first thing I wanted to get to, and you can tell us a little bit about your role at Microsoft, but I keep getting people asking me, Microsoft, they're like Enterprise, why are you talking to Microsoft at an app event?

9
00:01:01,040 --> 00:01:09,760
And so tell us a little bit about what you do, but then also like how the apps inside Microsoft are kind of a startup inside this massive company.

10
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:11,920
Yeah, thanks a lot for having me, David.

11
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:13,200
And hi, everyone.

12
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:15,040
And London is awesome, by the way.

13
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:20,960
So I'm part of product management at Microsoft 365 Consumer Business.

14
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:25,920
So basically our Word, Excel, PowerPoint apps and there's also the Microsoft 365 app.

15
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:31,200
So my current role is very focused on leading our apps on the Mac platform.

16
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:33,520
I mean, Mac App Store is a big part of it.

17
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:39,280
We also have a direct channel and then I support iOS and Android, basically our mobile apps as well.

18
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:45,360
And we sell Microsoft 365 Personal and Family, which is sort of our consumer subscriptions.

19
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:50,720
And then we also sell Copilot Pro and we have a fairly large business on App Store.

20
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:54,480
So like straight up in app purchase business on App Store and we are.

21
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,960
very much like you guys when it comes to just growing our apps.

22
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:05,360
And essentially, yes, Microsoft is an enterprise company in many ways, but we are also a very big consumer company.

23
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,400
Our app presence is actually pretty good.

24
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:09,680
So yeah.

25
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:16,400
If you look at a sensor tower or data AI, Microsoft apps are some of the leaders in monetization on the App Store.

26
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:18,720
Some of the biggest apps, some of the biggest performing apps.

27
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:24,880
And before talking to you, I kind of always thought of it like a lot of the questions I got this morning about why I was talking to Microsoft.

28
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:26,880
You think of it as like, oh, it's a afterthought.

29
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:28,880
It's something Microsoft does because they have to.

30
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:33,440
But the more you and I have gotten to know each other and talk through how Microsoft thininks about it.

31
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:41,440
It's pretty cool how you are able to operate as a startup and how much growth and monetization you are seeing on the app stores that it's not an afterthought.

32
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:43,520
It's actually like a huge line of business.

33
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:43,920
Yeah.

34
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:45,680
And it's a big part of the strategy.

35
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:59,920
So for our consumer business, app stores are very important and we actually have a pretty decent amount of people and money that we spend on all the things that are somewhat covered in this conference, like ASO, MMP.

36
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:01,600
We have a paid ad strategy.

37
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:03,040
We have a ASO strategy.

38
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:06,560
We have teams that are doing optimization on the funnel.

39
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:12,560
So at all parts, activation, monetization, acquisition and renewal optimization and so on.

40
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:17,840
I like to say we have an equivalent of say RevenueCat and Braze and some app companies inside.

41
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:20,720
Obviously, we work with a lot of vendors as well.

42
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:23,760
So yeah, we have a pretty decent strategy around that.

43
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:24,480
Awesome.

44
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:34,560
So I wanted to dive into ASO because I think, again, even though it's Microsoft, I think we all have a lot to learn from the strategies that you and your teams have employed there at Microsoft.

45
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:37,680
But before we go, I know you needed to make a small disclaimer.

46
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:38,160
Yes.

47
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:38,480
Yes.

48
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:42,720
So all opinions are my own and do not represent Microsoft's official stance.

49
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:43,680
So yeah.

50
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,240
So that is a disclaimer that I'm always supposed to make.

51
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:46,880
Yeah, of course.

52
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:47,520
And that's the thing.

53
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:48,960
Microsoft is such a big company.

54
00:03:49,280 --> 00:03:50,400
There's so many things going on.

55
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:57,440
It makes sense that we're going to talk about some of the things you've learned and stuff, but it's not official Microsoft strategy.

56
00:03:58,160 --> 00:04:07,520
But on ASO, I think a lot of people in this room, again, people I talked to this morning, think, oh, well, Microsoft can just own any keyword and like, oh, it's just all brand search.

57
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:09,200
Like, people just go and search Word.

58
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:12,080
But as you and I have discussed, that's not actually the case.

59
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:20,160
So tell me a little bit about what kind of ASO work Microsoft does to find the right audiences on the app stores.

60
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:20,640
Okay.

61
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:28,080
So I mean, just like most other app companies, we have an ASO vendor and we work with them on a number of things.

62
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:37,360
And so if you look at it broadly, there's the brand optimization which we can get because our apps are named Microsoft Word, Microsoft Excel, et cetera.

63
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:41,280
So like our brand is very much in the title itself most of the time.

64
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,640
So for the keyword optimization piece, we do competitive keywords.

65
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:51,360
So if you look at app stores, a lot of times we're not the number one productivity apps.

66
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:54,880
So there are other apps that are on Mac, on IOS, also on Android.

67
00:04:55,120 --> 00:04:58,000
There are a number of productivity apps that keep going up and down in the chart.

68
00:04:58,320 --> 00:05:00,560
So we have a competitive strategy with keywords.

69
00:05:00,720 --> 00:05:02,880
The app stores are kind of a great equalizer, right?

70
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:04,720
Yeah, it is a democratic place, right?

71
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:10,720
So it's not that if you are Microsoft, so you can just show up there and it's not an exclusive to Microsoft channel.

72
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:10,960
Right.

73
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:13,680
So like App Store really democratizes everyone.

74
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:24,800
Like just how Paramount Pictures and a YouTube influencer at the same stage when it comes to YouTube, like in terms of eyeballs, it's the same thing with App Store, like a new app can pretty much get the top spot.

75
00:05:25,360 --> 00:05:26,640
We have a competitive strategy.

76
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:29,760
So we always, for the most part, know who are the top competitors.

77
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:42,880
And sometimes actually when it comes to App Store, it's not even some of the names that you would think, like I mean, sure there are the big ones like Google, Canva, et cetera, but there are a lot of smaller competitors, smaller apps like productivity apps that are also on the top.

78
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:46,880
So we do have a strategy to get some of that keyword.

79
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:51,920
So competitive is a smaller part, but actually jobs to be done is a bigger part of the strategy.

80
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:54,720
So we have like generic and non-generic keywords.

81
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:58,080
So essentially keywords like notes, resumes.

82
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:04,320
We have a lot like user research around what are our apps used for because our apps are very general.

83
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:12,960
We try to focus on jobs to be done, like resume creation is a job to be done, note taking is a job to be done, slide creation of presentation decks.

84
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:17,200
Excel has budgeting as a big one, invoice creation.

85
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:19,840
So those are some of the jobs to be done that we know.

86
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:30,240
So we try to optimize for some of those keywords because somebody may be looking for invoice and may end up at Excel because not everyone is like a business owner looking for a QuickBooks type solution.

87
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:33,440
Sometimes you just need something very simple and Excel can do the job.

88
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:35,920
So those are the kind of things that we keep doing.

89
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:37,440
And there's no like constant set.

90
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:38,320
We are always.

91
00:06:38,640 --> 00:06:43,840
So every month we have an update and our paid ASA also kind of guides us.

92
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:51,440
And also there's this whole thing that once you're ranked, say top three in a keyword, we can remove that keyword and get new keywords in.

93
00:06:51,600 --> 00:07:02,880
If we're seeing that we are consistently ranked for like say a year or so, we can then, you know, slowly take that keyword out and put like an AI or a copilot because we also have like newer features and newer things coming in.

94
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:04,160
Like AI is a big one.

95
00:07:04,480 --> 00:07:06,560
So we try to rank for that and things like that.

96
00:07:07,040 --> 00:07:07,120
Yeah.

97
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:11,680
I think even for smaller and midsize apps, ASO is super competitive.

98
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:13,360
So Microsoft does have an advantage.

99
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:14,480
But I mean, you heard it here.

100
00:07:14,640 --> 00:07:21,120
Like on the app stores, you were competing with the biggest company in the world, a multi trillion dollar company in ASO.

101
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:30,480
And so the same strategies that Ramit at Microsoft is employing is what we all and I have apps on the app store as well, personally, in addition to working at RevenueCat.

102
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:34,400
And so it's the same kind of strategies we should all be employing, thinking through the jobs to be done.

103
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:36,640
And you were talking about app store search ads.

104
00:07:37,120 --> 00:07:49,040
And I want to touch briefly on how you look at the profitability of specific keywords and then how that helps inform your strategy, because I think that's something, you know, all app companies should be thinking about.

105
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:54,160
I mean, there's no one answer because at any given point of time we are running multiple campaigns.

106
00:07:54,800 --> 00:08:00,880
So like say if we are getting certain keyword that is really sticky, we can always bring it back.

107
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:04,960
But very basic like ASO 101, like the keyword bank is very small.

108
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:06,880
Like it's 100 characters.

109
00:08:07,280 --> 00:08:12,640
And then you have, I mean, yes, you can stuff a few things in the description, but it also has to make sense.

110
00:08:13,040 --> 00:08:14,800
So there is that aspect, that description.

111
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:15,920
has to be clean.

112
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:17,920
It has to reflect Microsoft.

113
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:21,520
We are not a junk app that we will write anything in the app store, right?

114
00:08:21,760 --> 00:08:30,000
So we have to make sure that everything that is external facing is like making a lot of sense and it's not just keyword optimization.

115
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:32,640
It's not just a bunch of keywords stuck together.

116
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:35,120
So there has to be some flow and logic to it.

117
00:08:35,520 --> 00:08:45,600
But yeah, if we do find from ASA, we find some things that are high performing, we can obviously bring it back to our general ASO like organic or we can just bid more there.

118
00:08:46,320 --> 00:08:52,800
And there's also the whole MMP angle there with ASO like trying to really make sense that if this is the keyword.

119
00:08:53,520 --> 00:09:04,640
that is only driving monthly active usage or is it driving real subscription because we have very strict CPI limits like cost per install limits that we want to kind of go after.

120
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:06,400
Yeah, I think that's a mistake.

121
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:12,080
A lot of folks make in their ASO strategy is going after keywords that are easy to rank for.

122
00:09:12,480 --> 00:09:21,200
And so like at Microsoft, because the apps get downloaded so much, you could probably rank for something like swimming, but it's like that's fine, but that's not going to drive business value.

123
00:09:21,360 --> 00:09:30,640
And so your ASO strategy shouldn't just be about ranking, but it should be about using app store search ads and other tools to understand the profitability of the specific keywords and being.

124
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:34,320
way more focused on your approach instead of just so scatter shot.

125
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:38,160
Do you see any differences between iOS and Android on that front?

126
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:39,280
Are there any differences there?

127
00:09:39,760 --> 00:09:39,840
Yeah.

128
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,040
I mean, there's the ATT 20 rule sort of thing.

129
00:09:43,280 --> 00:09:51,360
Like Android has a very large install base, but we constantly have seen and so Microsoft apps are available.

130
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:53,600
I believe in six plus app stores.

131
00:09:53,760 --> 00:09:57,200
So in China, we have a number of app stores that the apps are available in.

132
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:03,840
There are also pre install deals, but still Android monetizes way less than iOS does.

133
00:10:04,240 --> 00:10:06,000
So there's a ATT 20 sort of thing.

134
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:12,560
I mean, it's not exactly ATT 20, it's somewhere like that, that Android and app purchase revenue still lags pretty significantly to iOS.

135
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:26,960
And it becomes especially interesting from like a LTV to MAO perspective that for an Android user, for example, if you have to run a paid ads, our CPI limits are like fifty times less in some cases than iOS CPI limits, right?

136
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:40,160
Because basically it's like you can drive installs to Android and you can keep getting like more and more monthly active usage, but the percentage that converts to paying users is like super low.

137
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:46,480
So, so you have to be so careful with Android, both with cost per install, like what you pay from a paid ad, but also some.

138
00:10:46,480 --> 00:10:49,840
But also sometimes in terms of your organic strategies.

139
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:57,760
So for example, if you have an engagement platform that you are paying by, I don't know, data points, right?

140
00:10:58,160 --> 00:11:16,160
By data points or by usage or by MAU, you have to be really careful because your Android campaigns might start costing you more in terms of COGS because the LTV, because essentially if we are selling a consumer product, our LTVs are not like in thousands of dollars, they're in hundreds of dollars.

141
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:29,760
So we are just operating like any other consumer SaaS startup in that sense that we are making sure that our LTV to CAC ratios are kind of in range so that we don't overspend on in app engagement or on ads.

142
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:33,760
It's not that we are Microsoft so we'll just go and drain money, right?

143
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:39,120
Like so all the same restrictions apply and it ultimately comes to the LTV-CAC ratio.

144
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:39,360
Yeah.

145
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:46,560
Well, so I mean, I get asked this all the time, you know, oh, we've got millions of active users on Android, but it's just not monetizing.

146
00:11:46,720 --> 00:11:47,920
How do we improve monetization?

147
00:11:48,160 --> 00:11:52,880
Like I can now tell them, I talked to Rahmit from Microsoft and it's the same thing.

148
00:11:53,040 --> 00:11:56,880
It's just like Android doesn't monetize as well as iOS.

149
00:11:57,040 --> 00:12:02,240
And like understanding that reality can help you inform the Android strategy, but then also.

150
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:05,040
Android, I think we're going to see a lot of evolution.

151
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:15,600
I'm actually going to be talking to the Google Play Partnerships team on a forthcoming episode where we talk about some of the experiments that people are starting to find success with on Android.

152
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:26,720
But it's good to hear from a prioritization standpoint that, yeah, you can put a ton of work into it and Microsoft has and has all the resources, but it's just a different platform and it just monetizes differently.

153
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:35,360
And understanding that helps inform strategy of how you invest, how many data points you collect, how you spend on tools for Android versus iOS.

154
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:37,520
So it's super helpful to know.

155
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:49,200
Another thing I wanted to dive into was monetization and AB testing, and if there's any particular tests or anything that you think are especially interesting on subscription monetization.

156
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:49,600
Yeah.

157
00:12:50,240 --> 00:12:53,680
So before I get into that, I'll finish the Android versus iOS.

158
00:12:53,920 --> 00:12:58,800
Like, I'll just conclude it by saying that I think Android is a very promising platform.

159
00:12:59,920 --> 00:13:08,560
It's just that maybe developers need to think more than just straight up expensive subscriptions.

160
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:13,600
Like, because a lot of the subscriptions are very North America based.

161
00:13:13,920 --> 00:13:18,480
I think some of the price points may need to go down some new business model.

162
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:20,160
New business models may need to be there.

163
00:13:20,480 --> 00:13:24,240
Like there has to be much more thought that has to go in to monetize that platform.

164
00:13:24,560 --> 00:13:25,840
It's a harder platform to crack.

165
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,120
It's you can't just copy and paste your iOS or Android.

166
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:29,760
Yes, exactly.

167
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:35,520
It's just a harder platform to crack and there has to be more Android specific strategies that need to be applied.

168
00:13:36,080 --> 00:13:37,840
I'm not saying it's not a promising platform.

169
00:13:38,160 --> 00:13:41,760
I think we think it's a promising platform, but there's just more to be done there.

170
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:43,840
So now moving on to AB testing.

171
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:54,240
So yes, a lot of our teams do AB testing on mobile Microsoft 365 apps like Word, Excel, PowerPoint as well as the 365 app and other apps that we have in the family.

172
00:13:54,880 --> 00:13:56,400
We keep doing a number of experiments.

173
00:13:56,960 --> 00:14:12,960
And some of the things that have really worked, just like very plain bare bones ideas that have worked, I think CTAs, like if you have a free trial, make sure the CTA says it's a free trial, like try free for one month, something like that.

174
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:24,320
Because there's one thing that we have observed is that CTA has to really communicate the free value if it's available so that it's just more comfortable to press that button.

175
00:14:24,560 --> 00:14:24,720
Right.

176
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:32,720
So basically, like you have a free trial wording up on the top, but then you have, say, something that says buy now, for example.

177
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:45,280
People are just not comfortable because a lot of times, you know, because of how there's like a lot of this one click by type of things that have happened in the recent past, like people are very apprehensive of pressing the buttons, right?

178
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:48,640
Because they're like, oh, I'll just touch this and money will be charged.

179
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:54,720
So if you have a free trial, if you have a more comforting motion, like definitely make sure to have that on the CTA.

180
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:59,600
Then another thing we have found a lot of success with is cards and GIFs.

181
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,920
So instead of like lines of text using some kind of cards with GIFs.

182
00:15:04,240 --> 00:15:05,360
And what would be in the GIF?

183
00:15:05,680 --> 00:15:06,640
I pronounce it GIFs.

184
00:15:06,720 --> 00:15:07,280
GIFs, yeah.

185
00:15:07,360 --> 00:15:08,000
GIFs, GIFs.

186
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:08,880
GIFs, GIFs.

187
00:15:08,960 --> 00:15:09,680
GIFs, GIFs.

188
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:10,480
Potato, potato.

189
00:15:10,720 --> 00:15:11,680
What's in the GIFs?

190
00:15:11,680 --> 00:15:52,080
Like an animation and demonstrating the value almost like a little video kind of yeah sometimes that or sometimes just illustrations so for a Microsoft 365 subscription some of the more important things if you open any of our apps some of our more important things are like there is the premium apps that you get on desktop and then there's the one TB OneDrive storage that you get and then there's access to AI features that you get so those things like you can write them down or you can actually have like illustrations showing that you are getting you know one TB OneDrive for each of your family members so you'll have like six people showing and you see that one TB OneDrive is is going to all six of them.

191
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:56,400
So if you can show them more visually, it helps in converting better.

192
00:15:56,720 --> 00:15:59,680
So that's more of a kind of paywall optimization type of thing.

193
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:01,120
But yeah, no, that's a great tip.

194
00:16:01,280 --> 00:16:02,560
And I mean, I've been hearing that more.

195
00:16:02,880 --> 00:16:10,080
I actually I've been experimenting with a video on my paywall in my weather app and I've seen really good results from exactly what you're saying.

196
00:16:10,160 --> 00:16:12,080
It's like, yeah, people just don't read.

197
00:16:12,320 --> 00:16:14,720
So you can put all the great, like, copywriting you want.

198
00:16:15,200 --> 00:16:16,640
But it's like, you have to show them.

199
00:16:16,880 --> 00:16:16,960
Yeah.

200
00:16:17,360 --> 00:16:28,800
And in the age of TikTok and reels and everything else, like, what better way to show them than with animated video, you know, illustrations or other ways to like really capture their attention and then communicate.

201
00:16:29,520 --> 00:16:34,080
that value in a way that they probably won't read even if you write it very cleverly.

202
00:16:34,480 --> 00:16:35,600
So yeah, that's a great tip.

203
00:16:36,080 --> 00:16:40,000
Another thing you had mentioned was experimenting with just the ordering of subscriptions.

204
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:40,800
Yeah, yeah.

205
00:16:41,040 --> 00:16:44,640
So this was a very interesting experiment where we had our family and personal.

206
00:16:45,200 --> 00:16:53,200
So family is slightly more expensive, it's like 9,99 and personal is a 6,99 dollar subscription in the US and other region prices are different.

207
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:55,360
But so we just reordered them.

208
00:16:55,600 --> 00:16:58,000
So we made family as a default subscription.

209
00:16:58,480 --> 00:17:02,960
So personal used to be the default and then we reordered it and then we did one more change.

210
00:17:03,360 --> 00:17:05,040
So actually two separate AB tests.

211
00:17:05,040 --> 00:17:08,000
So one more change where we started showing monthlyly equivalent price.

212
00:17:08,080 --> 00:17:14,320
So like if it's 99 dollars for the year, then we just show something like 8 dollars or something like a monthly equivalent price.

213
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:15,600
So both of those were very successful.

214
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:20,320
So monthly equivalent price when people can see, oh, it's just going to cost me 8 dollars.

215
00:17:20,720 --> 00:17:24,160
Even if they have to be billed for the whole year, it's good.

216
00:17:24,480 --> 00:17:26,000
Like we saw success there.

217
00:17:26,240 --> 00:17:34,080
And the second one was, yes, this reordering where it was very interesting that when we reordered, both started selling more.

218
00:17:34,400 --> 00:17:39,200
And the one that went below the personal one, it actually started selling a lot more.

219
00:17:39,360 --> 00:17:44,640
So I think from a psychological perspective, when we were backtracking, maybe there was this whole idea.

220
00:17:45,360 --> 00:17:53,280
that people saw this default subscription, which was more expensive, and they're like, no, no, no, this is more expensive, but let me get this other one, which is cheaper.

221
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:56,640
But it kind of increased their purchase intent somehow.

222
00:17:57,360 --> 00:18:01,120
Now the question in that increase, sometimes this is very psychological.

223
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:12,800
Like sometimes when you see something expensive, like you walk into, say, like a Louis Vuitton store, you see a lot of these expensive things and then you end up buying a tie from there because, you know, it's the cheaper thing.

224
00:18:13,600 --> 00:18:15,680
But you still bought something from Louis Vuitton, right?

225
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:22,960
So it's kind of like that where you see this more expensive option up on the top and then you end up buying the cheaper one because you still bought something.

226
00:18:23,280 --> 00:18:23,360
Yeah.

227
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:26,400
And you still unlocked and you still have a premium experience.

228
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:26,560
Yeah.

229
00:18:26,880 --> 00:18:27,520
So something like that.

